netflix's latest star-studded drama the  trial of the chicago 7  written and directed by aaron sorkin  depicts the true story of eight  activists falsely accused of inciting  riots during the 1968 democratic  national convention now you might be  wondering to yourself  if there were eight on trial why isn't  it called the trial of the chicago eight  well i'll be covering all that and more  including the real-life characters  portrayed on screen  and also looking into whether some of  these events depicted in the film  actually happened like did abby and  jerry actually dress up as the judge and  wear police outfits under their gowns  but before we begin  make sure to like and subscribe so  you're notified as soon as one of my  videos comes out the film starts with  archival footage setting the stage for  the events to follow  mlk and bobby kennedy have been  assassinated the u.s is at war in  vietnam  and political and social unrest have  spread across the country the importance  of this context is highlighted by sasha  baron cohen's character abby hoffman  later in the film when justifying fellow  accused tom hayden  you can do anything to anything by  taking it out of context mr schultz this  trial doesn't make much sense without  framing it within the context of a  united states at war  divided and on the brink of social  change that's why sorkin begins the film  with actual footage to properly prime  the audience  and the parallels to modern day are  obvious as protests rage across the  streets  police engage in brutality and the  social political climate is at a tipping  point in fact the first draft of this  movie was written by sorkin in 2007  after steven spielberg approached him  about doing a movie on it but with both  men focused on  other projects it sat dormant for almost  a decade until the 2016 presidential  election  the resulting change spurred sorkin to  revisit the script and ultimately  directed himself  the first few minutes also set the stage  of the protests themselves  the democratic national convention was  in town and was a party divided you may  recall that the president at the time  was democrat lyndon b  johnson having been sworn into power on  november 22  1963 two hours after the assassination  of john f  kennedy seeing as he was the vice  president with a split democratic party  an increasing animosity for the war in  vietnam  he shocked the nation by announcing he  wouldn't run for re-election  and i will not accept the nomination of  my party for another term  as your president this pitted current  vice president hubert humphrey the  pro-war candidate against senator eugene  mccarthy who was anti-war  so thousands of protesters descended  upon chicago to protest the dnc's  nomination of a candidate who would be  just as pro-war as the republican  nominee richard nixon  there is simply not enough of a  difference between hubert humphrey and  richard nixon to make a difference  and so we're going to chicago these  protesters came from varying groups like  bobby seal head of the black panthers  who protested against an unjust war  sending thousands of african americans  to their death  tom hayden and reni davis who organized  students who saw their colleagues shift  off to war  david dellinger a pacifist who ran the  mobilization to end the war in vietnam  jerry rubin and abby hoffman leader of  the youth international party  also known as the yippies and finally  lee weiner and jon freund both  professors charged for their involvement  organizing the demonstrations the trial  would never have occurred if it weren't  for the transition of power from  democrat to republican  which saw former attorney general ramsey  clark appointed by jfk  replaced by john mitchell appointed by  president nixon fun fact john mitchell  was later found to be involved in the  watergate scandal  jon calls in u.s district attorney  richard schultz to invoke the rap  brown law a law originally passed by  southern whites  to limit the free speech of black  activists the law forbids conspiracy to  cross state lines to incite violence  schultz is caught off guard by this  request since former ag clark had the  fbi investigate the riots and found out  that it was actually the police who  instigated the violence but john  mitchell under the direction of  president nixon  wanted to show the nation that there  would be consequences for these protests  and the chicago seven would be tried for  their alleged crimes  schultz however sees this as a bad  decision as it would only give them a  bigger platform to spread their anti-war  agenda on top of everything else we're  giving them exactly what they want a  stage and an audience  for the state to successfully convict  they would have to prove these men  from varying groups and having different  political motivations  conspire together to incite violence and  the prosecution was  ruthless in its tactics including bobby  seal in the group  the head of the black panthers and  wanted for murder in connecticut a  murder he would later be found innocent  of  and speaking frankly the u.s attorney  wanted the negro defendant to scare the  jury i was thrown in  to make the group look scarier also the  scene where he was bound and gagged  actually happened and it didn't just  happen for one day it happened for  several  you can see a courtroom drawing  depicting this and this resulted in  defense lawyer william kunsler calling  the judge's courtroom quote a  medieval torture chamber bobby was  continually denied  access to his lawyer and later he'd be  given a mistrial leaving  seven to be tried and not eight john  mitchell's decision to indict these  members was also a personal one his  predecessor ramsay clark had apparently  resigned an  hour before mitchell's confirmation a  sign of disrespect so this was  mitchell's way of getting back at him  and why ramsay clark played by michael  keaton was so eager to take this stand  the jury never got to hear his testimony  however as the judge invoked voir dire a  separate hearing within the trial in  which the judge can determine whether  evidence is admissible not only that he  denied the defense's appeal to have the  jury notified that the former attorney  general was  questioned in the first place as the  film shows at the end 78  of chicago's attorneys had an  unfavorable opinion of judge  julius hoffman and they responded  overwhelmingly negative when asked if he  displayed an impartial attitude and  courtesy to both the prosecution and  defense  because the trial was deemed a farce  abby hoffman and jerry rubin would often  retaliate in kind  showing up one day in judges gowns with  police uniforms underneath not depicted  in the film was how abby would  continually call the judge  julie and one day he even brought in a  viet cong  flag which had to be wrestled out of his  hand all in all the defendants including  the lawyers received 170 citations of  contempt  ranging in prison time from two and a  half for winer  and four years and 18 days to lead  defense attorney william kunsler  these citations would later be thrown  out in the court of appeals as were the  prison sentences of the five  ultimately convicted of inciting  violence with weiner and throne being  acquitted the film also speaks to the  fractured nature of the democratic party  and one could argue how that party is  still fractured today  with progressives and neo-liberals  constantly at odds  there's no better encapsulation of this  than when tom and abby are trapped  against an upscale bar  as the rich democratic establishment  watches the convention  oblivious to the men and women  protesting outside to further hammer  home this point sorkin includes one  party member saying  am i the only one who sees what's going  on out there but even within this  progressive movement there are fractures  tom talks about how abby and the yippie  movement do a disservice to their cause  they aren't going to win over hearts and  minds by giving out flowers and  levitating the pentagon they need to be  taken  seriously and this seriousness and  respect for authority to a government  which looks at them with contempt  is something his character has to  overcome we see him stand up when the  judge leaves while the other accused do  not  at the end the judge tells him how  courteous and respectful he's been  during this whole process  and if his closing speech is brief and  well-mannered he'll take that into  account when delivering his sentence tom  needs to find the courage in himself to  stand up to a court and country which  has not shown him any respect  courage like he asked ramsay clark to  show in taking the stand  sir you have to find some courage now  he'll instead list off the names of the  over 4 700 american soldiers killed in  vietnam since the trial began five  months ago in protest of this sham  something even richard schultz stands up  for however the extent of his empathy  for the defense is something dramatized  by sorkin all in all the trial of the  chicago 7  shines a light on some of the political  issues we still face today  it's a scathing portrayal of the  criminal justice system and the  consolidation of power within political  parties the film asks us  how far are you willing to go to fight  for what you believe in  thanks for watching everyone be sure to  like and subscribe and share as i'm  always coming out with new videos  for more bad takes you can follow me on  twitter and instagram thingstoryt  until next time remember daddy loves you  very much
 

